  {
   "vo": "Chapter three. The rule that changed everything.",
   "chapter": "The Rule That Changed Everything",
   "scene": {
    "type": "statement",
    "kicker": "CHAPTER 3",
    "lines": [
     {
      "t": "THE RULE THAT"
     },
     {
      "t": "CHANGED EVERYTHING",
      "c": "gold"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "vo": "In twenty twenty-three, the IPL introduced the Impact Player. Each team can bring in a substitute during the match, which in practice means an extra batter, or an extra bowler. The jump in every chart we've seen starts that very season. Since then, the IPL has tried to help the bowlers: two bouncers an over from twenty twenty-four, and in twenty twenty-five, saliva back on the ball, and a second ball for dewy evenings.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "timeline",
    "head": {
     "t": "THE RULE BOOK",
     "sub": "IPL PLAYING CONDITIONS"
    },
    "events": [
     {
      "y": "2023",
      "t": "Impact Player introduced",
      "hl": true
     },
     {
      "y": "2024",
      "t": "Two bouncers per over"
     },
     {
      "y": "2025",
      "t": "Saliva back, second ball for dew"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "vo": "Compare the three seasons before it, with the four since. The run rate jumped from eight point three, to nine point five. Totals of two hundred went from one every five matches, to two in every three. And sixes rose from thirteen a match, to almost eighteen.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "compare",
    "head": {
     "t": "BEFORE AND AFTER",
     "sub": "IPL 2020-22 VS 2023-26"
    },
    "a": {
     "name": "2020-22",
     "color": "ink"
    },
    "b": {
     "name": "2023-26",
     "color": "gold"
    },
    "rows": [
     [
      "RUN RATE",
      "8.31",
      "9.52",
      "b"
     ],
     [
      "200+ TOTALS PER MATCH",
      "0.21",
      "0.67",
      "b"
     ],
     [
      "SIXES PER MATCH",
      "12.8",
      "17.6",
      "b"
     ]
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "vo": "But here's the twist. Teams don't actually use that extra batter much more. In twenty twenty-two, a number eight faced a ball in fifty-five per cent of innings. In twenty twenty-six, it was fifty-one per cent. And teams still lose about six wickets an innings, exactly as they did in twenty thirteen.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "stats",
    "head": {
     "t": "THE TWIST",
     "sub": "HOW DEEP TEAMS ACTUALLY BAT"
    },
    "tiles": [
     {
      "v": "55%",
      "l": "INNINGS WHERE NO. 8 FACED A BALL · 2022",
      "c": "ink"
     },
     {
      "v": "51%",
      "l": "SAME, 2026",
      "c": "gold"
     },
     {
      "v": "6.0",
      "l": "WICKETS PER INNINGS · SAME AS 2013",
      "c": "ink"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "vo": "So the Impact Player isn't extra batting. It's insurance. Knowing there's another batter waiting is what frees the top three to swing from ball one.",
   "scene": {
    "type": "statement",
    "lines": [
     {
      "t": "NOT EXTRA BATTING."
     },
     {
      "t": "INSURANCE.",
      "c": "gold"
     }
    ],
    "sub": "The extra batter rarely bats. Knowing he's there changes how everyone else plays."
   }
  },
  {
   "vo": "Chapter four. Two hundred is no longer safe.",
   "chapter": "200 Is No Longer Safe",
   "scene": {
    "type": "statement",
    "kicker": "CHAPTER 4",
    "lines": [
     {
      "t": "200 IS"
     },
     {
      "t": "NO LONGER SAFE",
      "c": "gold"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "vo": "Two hundred used to be a match-winning total. From twenty thirteen to twenty twenty-two, teams chasing two hundred or more won just ten times out of seventy-five. From twenty twenty-three to twenty twenty-five, about one in four. And in twenty twenty-six, seventeen out of thirty-eight. Almost half.",
